course: books
lesson: 55
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VIII, part 4: Toad's Adventures (the chase, the tunnel and the night wood)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The train rattles off with Toad in the engine's cab; fields, cows and hedges fly past, and he skips and sings, dreaming of supper, money and praise at Toad Hall — the strangest washerwoman the driver has ever seen.
explain: Then the driver hears another engine behind them, and in the moonlight he sees it crowded with warders waving halberds, policemen waving truncheons and detectives waving revolvers, all shouting «Stop, stop, stop!»
explain: Toad falls on his knees in the coal and confesses who he is; he says he only «borrowed» a motor-car, but the driver calls him a wicked toad — and still decides to help, because he dislikes motor-cars, bossy policemen and the sight of an animal in tears.
explain: The pursuers have the better engine, so the driver races through a long tunnel, brakes hard on the other side, and shouts «Now, jump!»; Toad rolls down the bank into a wood and watches the chasing engine roar past.
explain: He laughs for the first time since prison, but soon he remembers it is late, he has no money and no supper, and the wood is cold, dark and unfriendly: night-jars rattle, an owl brushes his shoulder, a fox teases him about missing socks, and at last he makes a bed of leaves in a hollow tree and sleeps till morning.
fact: Toad «eagerly scrambled up into the cab of the engine» (the cab = the driver's place); «The guard waved his welcome flag» and the train moved out.
fact: Thinking of Toad Hall, money and «praise and admiration at the recital of his adventures» (recital = telling), he «began to skip up and down and shout and sing».
fact: The driver had met washerwomen before, «but never one at all like this».
fact: The driver hears something: «we're the last train running in this direction to-night, yet I could be sworn that I heard another following us!»
fact: Toad feels «a dull pain in the lower part of his spine» — that is how fear feels to him.
fact: The chasing engine is full of «Men like ancient warders, waving halberds; policemen in their helmets, waving truncheons; and shabbily dressed men in pot-hats ... plain-clothes detectives ... waving revolvers and walking-sticks» (halberd = long pole with an axe-blade; truncheon = police club; pot-hat = bowler hat).
fact: Toad confesses: «I am not the simple washerwoman I seem to be! I have no children waiting for me ... I am a toad — the well-known and popular Mr. Toad, a landed proprietor» (a landowner).
fact: Asked why he was in prison, he says: «I only borrowed a motorcar while the owners were at lunch»: he still won't call it stealing.
fact: The driver: «I fear that you have been indeed a wicked toad, and by rights I ought to give you up to offended justice. But you are evidently in sore trouble and distress, so I will not desert you.»
fact: His reasons: «I don't hold with motor-cars», «I don't hold with being ordered about by policemen when I'm on my own engine», and «the sight of an animal in tears always makes me feel queer and softhearted» (don't hold with = don't like or approve of).
fact: The pursuers gain because «they are running light, and they have the better engine» (running light = with no carriages to pull).
fact: The plan: go full speed through the long tunnel, where the others «will slow down a bit ... for fear of an accident»; then brake, and Toad must «jump and hide in the wood, before they get through the tunnel and see you».
fact: Toad «jumped, rolled down a short embankment, picked himself up unhurt, scrambled into the wood and hid»; when the chase has passed he has «a hearty laugh — for the first time since he was thrown into prison».
fact: Night-jars (birds that make a whirring rattle at night) make him think «that the wood was full of searching warders»; an owl brushes his shoulder and he is sure «it was a hand».
fact: A fox says: «Hullo, washerwoman! Half a pair of socks and a pillow-case short this week! Mind it doesn't occur again!» Toad looks for a stone to throw, cannot find one, and is vexed (annoyed).
fact: Talk in this lesson: «gutted» (UK) = very disappointed; «yeet» = throw hard; «delulu» = believing something that isn't real; «ken» (Scotland) = know; «servo» (Australia) = petrol station.
exercise: Where does Toad ride on the train? | In the engine's cab, with the driver | In a first-class carriage with cushions | On the roof of the very last carriage | In the guard's van, among the parcels and the milk-churns
exercise: What does Toad do as the train speeds along? | Skips about, shouts and sings | Sits quietly and sleeps in the coal-dust | Helps the driver shovel coal into the fire | Cries the whole way, thinking of the dungeon
exercise: What is Toad thinking about when the trouble starts? | What to have for supper at home | How to wash the driver's shirts | How to say sorry to the girl | Whether to sell Toad Hall
exercise: What strange thing does the driver hear? | Another engine following them | A cow on the line ahead | A bell ringing in the tunnel | A voice singing in the reeds beside the line
exercise: Why is that strange? | They are the last train running that way tonight | Engines never run in the dark in those days | That railway line has only one rail and no room for two | The other engine has no driver at all
exercise: How does fear feel to Toad? | A dull pain low in his spine | A tickle in his long nose | A buzzing in both his ears | A great rumbling hunger in his empty tummy
exercise: Who is crowded on the chasing engine? | Warders, policemen and detectives | Badger, the Rat and the Mole | The gaoler's daughter and her aunt | Weasels, stoats and ferrets
exercise: What is a halberd? | A long pole with an axe-blade | A kind of loud police whistle | A small round shield | A short knife for cutting bread in the guardroom
exercise: What are they all shouting? | «Stop, stop, stop!» | «Poop-poop!» | «Off with his head!» | «Come back, Toady!»
exercise: What does Toad do when he sees them? | Falls on his knees and confesses who he is | Jumps off the train at once without a word | Hides under the coal and keeps very quiet | Pretends to be a sack of coal and says nothing at all
exercise: What does Toad call himself in his confession? | A landed proprietor | A poor washerwoman | A famous engine-driver | A sailor from the sea
exercise: How does Toad describe what he did with the motor-car? | He only «borrowed» it while the owners were at lunch | He says he bought it and paid in gold sovereigns | He says the owners gave it to him as a present, with a bow on it | He says he never even saw a motor-car in his life
exercise: What does the driver first say about Toad? | That he has been a wicked toad | That he is the bravest toad alive | That he is a fine driver | That he is a good washerwoman
exercise: Which is one of the driver's reasons for helping Toad? | He doesn't like motor-cars | He wants a big reward of money | Toad is his cousin from Town | He is afraid of Toad's lawyers
exercise: Why are the pursuers gaining on them? | They run light and have the better engine | They are going downhill all the way | They have twice as many men to shovel the coal | Their engine has a sail as well, and the wind is behind it
exercise: Why will the pursuers slow down in the tunnel? | For fear of an accident | Because the tunnel is too narrow for them | Because they stop for a cup of tea | Because their lamps go out in the dark
exercise: What must Toad do when the driver brakes? | Jump and hide in the wood | Pull the brake handle himself | Wave a white flag at the others | Climb onto the roof and lie flat
exercise: What happens to Toad when he jumps? | He rolls down a bank and is unhurt | He breaks his leg and cannot walk | He lands in a river and has to swim | He is caught at once by a policeman
exercise: What does Toad do after the chasing engine roars past? | Has a hearty laugh | Bursts into tears | Runs after it shouting | Goes back to the line
exercise: Why does he soon stop laughing? | It's late, cold and dark, and he has no money or supper | He sees the policemen climbing back down the bank | He finds he has lost the washerwoman's bonnet somewhere in the tunnel | The engine-driver comes back to fetch his shirts
exercise: What do the night-jars' rattling calls make Toad think? | That warders are closing in on him | That a train is coming back again | That his friends are calling him | That it is going to rain hard all night long
exercise: What does the owl do that frightens Toad? | Brushes his shoulder with its wing | Pecks him hard on the nose | Steals his bonnet | Hoots his name aloud, «Toad! Toad!», from a branch
exercise: What does the fox say to Toad? | «Half a pair of socks and a pillow-case short this week!» | «Give me your money, or I'll call the police at once!» | «I know who you are, Mr. Toad of Toad Hall!» | «Follow me, and I'll take you safely home to your friends on the river bank!»
exercise: Where does Toad sleep that night? | In a hollow tree, on branches and leaves | On a bench in the next station | In a farmer's barn full of hay | Under the railway bridge, wrapped in the washerwoman's shawl
exercise: Looking back: in chapter III, where did the Mole hide when he was terrified in the Wild Wood at night? | In the hollow of an old beech tree | Under a heap of snow by a door-mat | In a rabbit burrow with a friendly rabbit | In Badger's coal-cellar
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I, which animals did the Rat say «break out sometimes», so «you can't really trust them»? | Weasels, stoats and foxes | Otters, kingfishers and moorhens | Rabbits and squirrels | Ducks and swans
exercise: Looking back: in chapter VI, where were the owners when Toad took their car? | Eating lunch in the inn's coffee-room | Asleep in their beds upstairs | Buying petrol at the garage | Walking down by the river with their big dog after the long drive
exercise: ★★★ Why must Toad jump AFTER the tunnel, not before it? | So he gets out before the chasers come through and can see him | Because the wood is only on the far side, and the chasers would still pass | Because there is no wood at all before the tunnel | Because the train cannot slow down before a tunnel
test: «Toad was gutted the driver grassed him up.» (gutted = very disappointed; grassed up = told on) Put it right. | The driver did not give him up; he helped him escape | Yes, the driver stopped the train for the police | The driver threw him off and drove away laughing | The driver locked him in the coal-box for the police
test: Which is TRUE about the driver? | He thinks Toad was wicked, yet helps him | He thinks Toad did nothing wrong at all | He is really a policeman in disguise | He wants Toad to drive the engine
test: «I only borrowed a motorcar.» What is the truth? | He stole it: he took it without asking and was found guilty | He did borrow it, and gave it back the next day | He bought it from the owners at the inn | The owners lent it to him for a race and said he could keep it if he won
test: «Toad yeeted a stone at the fox.» (yeet = throw hard) Put it right. | He looked for a stone but could not find one | He threw a big stone and hit the fox | He threw his rusty black bonnet at the fox and missed | He threw the fox into a river
test: Kirsty from Fife: «Toad's delulu, he kens fine the owl's a warder's hand.» (delulu = believing something unreal; kens = knows) Put it right. | It was only an owl's wing; Toad's fear made him imagine a hand | It really was a warder's hand reaching for him | It was the fox's paw on his shoulder | It was a low branch of the hollow tree, swaying in the night wind
test: What would have happened if Toad had jumped while the chasers could see him? | They would have stopped and caught him in the wood | They would have waved and gone home | The driver would have been given a medal | Nothing, because chasers never look out of engines
test: Why does the fox call Toad «washerwoman»? | Toad is still in the washerwoman's gown and bonnet | The fox knows Toad washes shirts for money | Toad is carrying a big bundle of washing | The fox has mixed him up with Badger in his long dressing-gown
test: «Half a pair of socks» — what is the joke? | Half a pair is one sock, as if the washerwoman lost one | Socks come in halves in the country | The fox has only one foot | It means a very small sock knitted for a baby fox
test: Liam from Brisbane: «they stopped the train at the servo and Toad just walked off.» (servo = petrol station) Put it right. | The driver slowed after a tunnel and Toad jumped into a wood | The train stopped at a big station and Toad got off | Toad jumped into a river from a bridge | Toad hid under a seat in a carriage and got off right at his own station
test: Looking back: in chapter III the Rat said Toad wouldn't go into the Wild Wood alone «for a whole hatful of golden guineas». Where is Toad tonight? | Alone in a strange wood at night, frightened by every sound | Safe in bed at Toad Hall | Rowing on the river with the Rat | At Badger's house, with a red handkerchief
test: Looking back: what did the Rat shout at the motor-car that wrecked the caravan in chapter II? | «You villains! ... you — roadhogs!» | «Come back and give us a ride!» | «Poop-poop! O bliss!» | «Mind the ducks, you careless fellow!»
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, part 1, Toad praised his friends in the cell. Now, in his confession, what does he call himself? | «the well-known and popular Mr. Toad» | «a stupid and forsaken animal» | «a poor humble washerwoman» | «a friend of the engine-driver»
test: Looking back: in chapter VII the song in the reeds said «I spring the trap that is set». Who gets Toad out of his trap tonight? | A kind engine-driver who helps him jump | The Piper himself, playing his pipes | The Rat and the Mole in their boat | The gaoler's daughter riding on the engine
test: ★★★ Count: how many kinds of men are on the chasing engine? | Three | Two | Four | Five
test: ★★★ Careful: the pursuers have «the better engine», yet Toad escapes. What makes up for the slower engine? | A clever plan: speed in the tunnel, then a quick stop and jump | Toad's own engine suddenly becomes the faster one | The pursuers run out of coal before the tunnel | The pursuers take a wrong turning at a junction
test: ★★★ The driver gives three reasons for helping. Which is NOT one of them? | He wants Toad to pay him in gold | He doesn't like motor-cars | He doesn't like being ordered about by police on his engine | Tears make him feel softhearted
exam: Identify the persons by whom the engine of the prisoner was pursued. | Warders bearing halberds, constables bearing truncheons, and detectives in plain clothes | A party consisting of the Badger, the Water Rat and the Mole, in a borrowed motor-car | The owners of the stolen motor-car, accompanied by their chauffeur | The gaoler's daughter and her aunt, the latter having freed herself from her bonds and raised the alarm
exam: State the principal reason adduced by the engine-driver for the pursuers' advantage in speed. | That they were running light, possessing in addition the better engine | That the gradient of the line was in their favour throughout | That their fireman was more industrious in the stoking of coal | That the prisoner's weight retarded the progress of his engine
exam: Ascertain the stratagem by which the prisoner's escape from the train was effected. | Rapid passage through a tunnel, followed by braking and a leap into the adjoining wood | The uncoupling of the carriages in order to obstruct the line behind | A concealment of the prisoner within the tender among the coals until the pursuers had passed by | The prisoner's descent at a station where the pursuers were not expected
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the prisoner's night in the wood? | He took shelter in a hollow tree and slept soundly until morning | He was apprehended by the fox and conveyed to the authorities | He walked without rest until he arrived at Toad Hall at dawn | He passed the night upon the railway embankment in full view of all who travelled along the line
